ve been  and you will concede there have been  significant cuts to ministry of justice  budget to the cps to frontline policing  to activity in the courts that those  cuts have had not had any effect on the  conviction rates  so first of all let's get the numbers  very straight since 2010 between 2010  and 2016 the moj budget the spend was  reduced by roughly a quarter that was us  dealing with the aftermath of the  economic crisis and the mess that the  labour party made of it from 2017 to  current day we increased the budget by  nine percent but it's not just about  money so for example if you look at uh  one of the measures that we're taking to  come through uh the backlog and in the  civil law system things like probate and  divorce we've had tremendous success in  a driving efficiency and better service  for those that use the justice system is  digitization and technology so yes  investment is important it's increased  by nine percent in my department since  2017 but there are other things it's the  way we do things that matter as well and  i think that's true for the experience  of whether it's witnesses victims or the  wider public of what they see and feel  of the criminal justice system  but just to pick you up in a specific  sentence construction you just used  there that it is not just to do with the  money i would agree with that it just  seems though that kit malt house  yesterday the prime minister yesterday  suggested that it's nothing to do with  the cut in the budgets that we've seen  at the mlg  well we've had an increase in the budget  by nine percent since 2017 so i'm making  the point that of course uh it's  important to invest in the right areas  but you've got to retain a responsible  approach to the public finances all  together and what i'm also saying and  that i've got the advantage as you said  neil coming back to the moj um i was  courts minister in 2018.  let me give you three example gps  tagging  sobriety tags and virtual  giving of 